  "slug": "genome-assembly-e9c4",
  "summary": "Phased and telomere-to-telomere assemblies now extend from centromeres to population-scale variation across human, East Asian, lotus and conifer genomes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that contiguous eukaryotic assemblies now serve as references for variant-level and evolutionary inference. Two human-focused preprints deliver phased resources: 6,312 complete centromeres from 320 phased genomes, with HOR arrays, pericentric inversions and population stratification, and 320 near-complete East Asian assemblies from 160 individuals revealing novel sequences, gene gains and structural variants. Two telomere-to-telomere Nelumbo assemblies are paired with population analysis of 832 accessions to address domestication and precision breeding. The Cupressus sempervirens chromosome-scale assembly (10 Gb, contig N50 29.8 Mb, 42,980 genes) and the Nigella damascena genome link TE-driven intron expansion, LTR accumulation and genomic gigantism to trait and conifer evolutionary history. What is new is the pairing of contiguous references with variant-level analysis across many accessions, most evident in the human and Nelumbo studies.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-29"
}
